WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:04.380
If you thought Justice League was a mess,
it's important to remember things could've

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.220
been far, far worse.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:12.170
Back in 1997, Joel Schumacher's Batman &amp; Robin
nearly ruined comic book movies forever.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.960
"Alright everyone, chill."

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.040
But surprisingly enough, it wasn't the worst
comic adaptation of that year.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.050
CBS took a shot on piloting a TV series called
Justice League of America … but the results

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.980
made Batman &amp; Robin look like The Dark Knight.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.560
"Why so serious."

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.070
The premise was simple.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.900
In fact, a little too simple.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.321
It centered on Matthew Settle as Green Lantern,
alongside Kenny Johnston's The Flash, John

00:00:38.321 --> 00:00:41.380
Kassiras The Atom, and Michelle Hurd as Fire.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:45.050
These are all characters that have been associated
with Justice League in the comics, but the

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.440
kicker in this adaptation was that they were
all roommates and regular people with ordinary

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.440
problems.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.579
It's as if CBS asked for DC's Super Friends
meets NBC's Friends.

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:55.890
"WHAT IS THE MATTER WITH YOU?"

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.320
In fact, it'd be shocking if that exact phrase
didn't come up during a pitch meeting.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.050
Justice League of America turned out to be
a weird amalgam of an action-drama and a sitcom,

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.240
except the special effects were embarrassing
even by '90s standards and the comedy was

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.240
downright painful.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.720
There were also extremely awkward interview
segments with the characters scattered throughout,

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.280
giving the whole thing a quasi-mockumentary
feel that interrupted any semblance of flow

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.280
it might've had.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.170
But a lot of the problems came from the would-be
series botching the iconic characters at their

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:26.170
cores.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:29.820
Green Lantern was shown as a software salesman
and was based on Guy Gardner from the comics

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.970
while looking more like the Kyle Rayner version
and being as dull as Hal Jordan from the Super

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:34.970
Friends cartoon.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:39.010
"If it's a joust you want, it's a joust you'll
get!"

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:44.380
Meanwhile, The Flash was depicted as struggling
with unemployment despite his super-speed,

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.190
The Atom was a science teacher who could shrink
himself small enough to fix an old-school

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:53.090
TV, and Fire was a struggling actress who
definitely dressed up as a banana for a commercial

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:54.090
audition.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:58.090
Then there was Martian Manhunter, played by
David Ogden Stiers from M*A*S*H, whose face

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:02.120
was always covered with a budget version of
what Jim Carrey wore The Mask.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:07.170
Although that might all sound a little like
some hokey remake of The Incredibles, it wasn't.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.769
"Man, it wasn't good enough."

00:02:09.769 --> 00:02:14.139
Each character was unlikable in his or her
own way, and the plot was even worse.

00:02:14.139 --> 00:02:18.900
In the episode, the team recruits a new member
in Ice, who started out as a meteorologist

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.209
in the town of….

00:02:20.209 --> 00:02:21.209
New Metro.

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:25.420
Yes, that was the actual, completely uninspired
name of a city from the show.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.090
After a freak lab accident gave Ice the ability
to freeze things, she worked with the other

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.980
heroes to take down the villainous Weatherman,
played by the late, great Miguel Ferrer.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.709
Since the show didn't get past the pilot,
it's not clear if the plan was to feature

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:43.040
a new villain in each episode, but Ferrer
does a serviceable job in one of his trademark

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.040
bad guy roles.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.269
But even that wasn't enough to redeem it.

00:02:46.269 --> 00:02:49.680
The biggest problem with Justice League of
America was that it couldn't figure out if

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.049
it was a sitcom or something more serious.

00:02:52.049 --> 00:02:55.809
While modern shows like Arrow, Supergirl,
and Marvel's Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:02:55.809 --> 00:02:59.810
find subtle ways to use humor as a balance
for the darker moments, this show went way

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.239
too far into family-friendly cheese to ever
make anything seem consequential.

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:08.120
Justice League of America never hit the air
in the U.S., although some overseas markets

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.120
may have seen it.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.069
However, the pilot still has life thanks to
the wonders of the internet and bootleg screenings

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:14.819
shown at comic book conventions.

00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:18.650
But it's not a cult following that's developed
in the wake of its modern availability; it

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:23.290
mostly just serves as a cautionary tale about
half-heartedly adapting beloved comic book

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:26.719
characters when you have little to no budget
or creative vision.

00:03:26.719 --> 00:03:29.680
"I've just pushed people and run away"

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.480
We were all into different stuff two decades
ago, but it's difficult to believe anyone

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.370
would've ever been into this.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:39.370
Although Zack Snyder's Justice League didn't
do as well as expected at the box office and

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.470
critics were certainly not kind, it looks
like a legitimate masterpiece compared to

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.470
this show.

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.099
"It has to."

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:47.890
Thanks for watching!

